{"headline":"Great week — your legs and chest led the way with solid strength gains across the board.","summary":"This was a productive period for muscle building. You hit 28 total sets across all major muscle groups, generating over 10,700 kg of total tonnage — a healthy output for a 28-year-old male at your body weight. Legs and chest showed the clearest strength improvements, and back continued its steady climb. Shoulders, biceps, and triceps held steady without regressing, which is fine, though there is room to push those groups harder in the coming period. Overall, you are trending in the right direction for your goal of building muscle.","progressiveOverload":{"status":"on_track","notes":"Chest moved from 104 to 106, legs from 105 to 107, and back from 103 to 104 — all meaningful single-period gains that confirm progressive overload is working on your primary movers. Shoulders held flat at 103, biceps at 102, and triceps at 104, meaning those groups neither progressed nor regressed this period. Consistent with last period's recommendation to maintain current progression, the main lifts are responding well. The accessory groups are the next frontier to nudge forward."},"muscleImbalances":[{"muscleGroup":"biceps","severity":"mild","observation":"Biceps represent only 4% of total volume and showed no strength index improvement this period. Given that back sits at 28% of volume, the pulling chain may be under-taxing the elbow flexors — a modest volume bump could restore balance."},{"muscleGroup":"shoulders","severity":"mild","observation":"Shoulders account for just 10.4% of volume with a flat strength index, sitting notably lower than chest at 26.8%. For long-term shoulder health and aesthetic balance, gradually increasing shoulder set count is advisable."}],"deloadRecommended":false,"deloadReason":null,"actionItems":["Add one extra set to both shoulders and biceps next period, bringing shoulders to 5 sets and biceps to 4, to address the mild volume imbalance and give those strength indices a chance to climb.","Continue the current loading progression on chest and legs — these groups are responding well and should not be changed; simply add weight or reps wherever the last session felt manageable.","Focus on back-to-biceps transfer: consider finishing back sessions with a dedicated curl variation to better activate the biceps through a fresh stimulus rather than as a standalone afterthought.","Log your sessions consistently so routine adherence data can be captured — having that information next period will allow for a much sharper analysis of which days and exercises are driving your progress."],"contextSummary":"Solid across-the-board week with chest, legs, and back all gaining strength index points; shoulders and arms held flat and need a small volume nudge next period."}